parents at a local charter school are  searching for answers after they say  students ate marijuana edibles on campus  and parents were not immediately  notified this happened friday at quest  preparatory academy near craig and  rancho in the northwest valley kate  houston is live in studio after speaking  to a mom who says there needs to be  better emergency protocols here kate yes  parents say a student brought the  edibles to school and gave them to  several other kids some knew what they  were eating others didn't the mother i  spoke to says the school did not  immediately contact parents and  paramedics were not called quest  preparatory academy serves students  kindergarten through eighth grade janae  simultan says her daughter was one of  the students that ate an edible but was  not told until after that it contained  marijuana simpleton says this happened  in the morning and parents didn't find  out until hours later at school pickup  simultan's daughter has asthma she  doesn't understand why the kids did not  receive medical attention as a parent we  can't help to think about the  possibilities of what could have  happened you had a kid fall asleep on  the table my daughter fell asleep on the  floor in her backpack  he had another kid walk out crying  another kid thrown up  those are all signs for me that  medical officials should have definitely  been contacted and they were not  simultan says she tried to speak to the  principal but says she was told the  principal was not available due to a  holiday party she says they did  communicate hours later but she wants  more to be done to ensure this doesn't  happen again simpleton plans to contact  the state public charter school  authority which oversees quest  preparatory academy  and i have reached out to quest  preparatory academy as well as the state  public charter school authority for some  more information on their safety  protocols when minors ingest drugs i  have not yet heard back sasha
 

